{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015EM5bwhdBuG1iXexo1226q","type":"tool_result","content":"8956:    if (this.voice && Math.floor(this.shown) > prev && (this._blip++ % 2 === 0)) this.game.audio.sfx(this.voice);\n12953:    this.game = game; this.song = o.song; this.lanes = o.lanes || ['left', 'down', 'up', 'right']; this.windows = Object.assign({ perfect: 40, great: 80, good: 120 }, o.windows); this.lead = o.lead || 1.6; this.voice = Object.assign({ wave: 'square', vol: .2, dur: .09 }, o.voice); this.on = o.on || {};\n13015:      if (n.call) { if (!n.played && (n.step - s) * spb < .1) { n.played = true; A.sfx(Object.assign({ freq: CALL[n.lane % CALL.length] }, this.voice), { step: n.step }); } if (!n.j && s >= n.step) { n.j = 'call'; this._flash[n.lane] = this.game.time; } continue; }\n- `E.shapeMesh({ seg })`: seg(r): the segments round a radius r (default 8 to 24)\n- `E.shapeMesh() → groups`: each one's mat (the material ready to draw: E.material's), key (its id), lid (true: the faces looking up or down, which some patterns draw their own way), curved (turned or round faces: planks run up them), face (a pattern drawn once per face, the crate's sides and bark's ends: uv runs 0 to 1 across each face), pos, nrm, uv (Float32Arrays: three numbers a corner, three corners a triangle; \n- `E.shapeSprite(id, o = {})`: a shape as a sprite (E.sprite's kind: r.sprite, px.sprite, inventories, HUDs, portraits): E.shapeSprite(id, { view: 'iso', facing, size (its scale, 1), mats, params, outline: true | a color | false }). The view is a name or a View, as given (r.shape draws steep views from the characters' angle: E.charView(view) gives that); a bag icon is the item's shape seen at an angle. r.sprite stand\n\n## 21. Ui\n\n- `new E.Dialog(game, o = {}) → talk`: new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, place, color, nameColor, voice, bg, border, vars }): a dialog box that types its text; say() opens it, update(dt) and draw(r) run it\n- `talk.choiceAt(i)` †: where choice i is on the screen while the choices show: [x, y] (its middle, for a test's tap), else null\n- `talk.close()`: close it now (and forget pending presses, so the same press doesn't also attack)\n- `talk.draw(r)`: draw it (anywhere in draw: it goes to the overlay)\n- `talk.pageLen`: the letters on the page shown\n- `talk.say(text, o = {})`: open with one string or a list of strings (each starts a new page). opts: { name, onDone, choices, onChoice, portrait: rig (a live close-up of that Humanoid beside the text) or a function (g, x, y, size), portraitSize: 40, portraitOpts: { zoom, turn, bg }, auto: seconds a page shows before it turns by itself, modal: false (play goes on while it shows: radio chatter), place: 'top' | 'middle\n- `talk.typing`: true while the page is still typing\n- `talk.update(dt)`: type and turn pages (confirm finishes the line, then turns the page); returns true while open and modal, so gameplay can wait: if (talk.update(dt)) return;\n- `talk.auto`: seconds each page stays after typing\n- `talk.bg = '#141a3a'`, `talk.border = '#e8e4ff'`: the box's colors\n- `talk.choice = 0`: the choice under the cursor\n- `talk.choices`: the answers offered on the last page, or null\n- `talk.color = '#ffffff'`: the text's color\n- `talk.game`: its game\n- `talk.i = 0`: the page shown\n- `talk.lines = 3`: text lines per page\n- `talk.modal`: false lets play go on (radio chatter)\n- `talk.name`: the speaker's name shown on the box\n- `talk.nameColor = '#ffd36a'`: the speaker's name's color\n- `talk.onChoice`: called with the index picked\n- `talk.onDone`: called when the last page closes\n- `talk.open = false`: true while it shows\n- `talk.pages = []`: the text wrapped into pages of lines\n- `talk.place = 'bottom'`: 'bottom' | 'top' | 'middle'\n- `talk.portrait`: the portrait beside the text (a Humanoid, or a function (g, x, y, size)), or null; pSize: its size in pixels; pOpts: its options ({ zoom, turn, bg })\n- `talk.shown = 0`: letters typed so far on it\n- `talk.speed = 50`: letters typed per second\n- `talk.vars = null`: the values {$name} in the text reads (say's vars first)\n- `talk.voice = 'text'`: the sound effect each letter blips ('text'; null for silence)\n- `new E.Menu(game, items, o = {}) → menu`: Menu with a cursor (title screens, pause menus, shops, battle commands). const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', 'OPTIONS', { label: 'CONTINUE', disabled: !save }], { onPick(i, item) {...}, onCancel() {...} }); update: menu.update(dt) draw: menu.draw(r) opts: { x: 'center' | px, y: 'center' | px, title, box: true, color, pick: '#ffd36a', columns: 1 }\n- `menu.draw(r)`: draw it to the overlay\n- `menu.selected`: the entry under the cursor\n- `menu.update()`: move the cursor (up, down; held keys repeat; the mouse over an entry) and pick (confirm, or a click or tap on an entry) or cancel; returns true when it picked or cancelled\n- `menu.active = true`: false stops it reading input\n- `menu.bg = '#141a3a'`, `menu.border = '#e8e4ff'`: the window's colors\n303:- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\n459:- `new E.Game({ menu })`: the pause, settings and controls screens ({ items, settings, controls, quit } adjusts them, false leaves them out)\n1163:- `new E.Shell(game, o = {}) → shell`: new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings', meters: .125, standard: true }): an HTML interface over the game (see the section's header)\n1166:- `new E.Shell({ settings })`: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max, step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard ones)\n1181:- `shell.show(name, spec = {})` †: open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (strings or functions), actions: [[label, fn] or { label, fn, act, primary, disabled }], back (fn for cancel) }. From another screen it stacks (back() returns); shown again, it updates (the new spec over the old). 'settings' takes its items\n53:- *NES typography.* Use the default 5x7 font with a drop shadow for HUD text, `E.ui.box` gradient panels and bars, and `E.font.title` with a 3-4 color gradient and extrusion for logos. A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild, Arkham) wants an HTML HUD instead: `E.Shell` (below), as the STEALTH slice and the template game do; its dialogue stays `E.Dialog` with portraits.\n84:- **Pause menu** (full engine): a pausable scene pauses on Escape, P, Start, the touch START button or the corner's menu button, and shows Resume, your items, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help (when your `E.Shell` has one) and Quit to title (it asks first; Keep playing has the focus). Arrows, d-pad and stick move, Enter or A chooses, Escape or B goes back one screen, P or Start closes the menu. The game sees none of those presses.\n110:- Your own `E.Shell` takes over from the engine's and keeps the standard menu (`standard: false` leaves it out).\n311:### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n312:A modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n313:- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n660:- The camera follows the player and the level is visible. The HUD uses screen pixels (`r.text`, `r.overlay`), anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`, or for a modern game `E.Shell`'s HTML pieces (`ui.hud` every step). With `expand: true`, look at it on a wide monitor and on a phone both ways (`tools/check.mjs` takes `--size 390x844@3`).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8704baa7-6f68-414d-afec-f55fd2d8121c","uuid":"4ae89f43-d3e4-43d8-866b-752d53059199","timestamp":"2026-11-03T01:38:28.801Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"8956:    if (this.voice && Math.floor(this.shown) > prev && (this._blip++ % 2 === 0)) this.game.audio.sfx(this.voice);\n12953:    this.game = game; this.song = o.song; this.lanes = o.lanes || ['left', 'down', 'up', 'right']; this.windows = Object.assign({ perfect: 40, great: 80, good: 120 }, o.windows); this.lead = o.lead || 1.6; this.voice = Object.assign({ wave: 'square', vol: .2, dur: .09 }, o.voice); this.on = o.on || {};\n13015:      if (n.call) { if (!n.played && (n.step - s) * spb < .1) { n.played = true; A.sfx(Object.assign({ freq: CALL[n.lane % CALL.length] }, this.voice), { step: n.step }); } if (!n.j && s >= n.step) { n.j = 'call'; this._flash[n.lane] = this.game.time; } continue; }\n- `E.shapeMesh({ seg })`: seg(r): the segments round a radius r (default 8 to 24)\n- `E.shapeMesh() → groups`: each one's mat (the material ready to draw: E.material's), key (its id), lid (true: the faces looking up or down, which some patterns draw their own way), curved (turned or round faces: planks run up them), face (a pattern drawn once per face, the crate's sides and bark's ends: uv runs 0 to 1 across eac